<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paleo-Indians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Paleoindians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paleo-Indians were the first peoples to enter and inhabit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, towards the end of the Late Pleistocene. Traditional theories hold that big-animal hunters crossed from North Asia over the Beringia land bridge, which existed from 45,000 to 12,000 BCE, and moved south once ice-free corridors opened along the Pacific coast and valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; the dates and routes remain debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a>, above all projectile points and scrapers, are the main evidence of their lives, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> culture appeared around 11,500 BCE. As the climate stabilised from 8000 to 7000 BCE, the more sedentary Archaic Period followed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term Paleo-Indian was first coined by Frank H. H. Roberts in 1940, to describe artifacts rather than peoples. Hannah Marie Wormington was the first, in 1957, to use it for the first inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, those who had lived there before about 6000 years ago, alongside now-extinct animals, and who made fluted stone points. The word applies to the lithic period of the Western Hemisphere and is distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>.</p><p>Researchers continue to discuss the dates and routes of the migration. The traditional theory holds that migrants moved into Beringia about 17,000 years ago, when glaciation lowered sea levels, and followed herds of megafauna along ice-free corridors between the Laurentide and Cordilleran ice sheets. An alternative scenario has them travelling down the Pacific coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, on foot or by boat; evidence of that route would have been submerged by a sea-level rise of more than a hundred metres. Conventional estimates place humans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> between 15,000 and 20,000 years ago, though some groups may have reached South America as early as 25,000 years ago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Paleo-Indians lived in highly mobile bands of about 20 to 60 members of an extended family. Small bands hunted and gathered in spring and summer and split into smaller family groups for the autumn and winter; family groups moved every three to six days, possibly travelling up to 360 km a year.</p><p>Their tools included fluted spear points, made by chipping a single flake from each side to leave a long groove, and microblades for butchering and processing hides. Projectile points and hammerstones were traded or moved long distances, from North Dakota to Wyoming and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> interior to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. From about 10,500 to 9,500 BCE the hunters of the Great Plains came to focus on a single species, an early bison; the earliest known such tradition is Folsom, whose people returned each year to the same springs on higher ground.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> culture takes its name from Clovis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, where in 1936 distinctive points were found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackwater-draw">Blackwater Draw</a> directly associated with the bones of Pleistocene animals. Until recently it was generally believed that the first people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> belonged to Clovis. Newer finds, including Cooper's Ferry in Idaho, Cactus Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, Meadowcroft Rockshelter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and Monte Verde in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, have produced early dates that lead researchers to re-examine the time range of Clovis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paleo-Indians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
